.TH PROGRAM_ALL_FPGA_AL9.SH 1 "2026-10-06" "otsdaq-mu2e 11.03.00" "Mu2e DAQ Manual"
.SH NAME
program_all_FPGA_AL9.sh \- load one bitstream into the FPGA behind every
USB JTAG cable on this node, then reset PCIe
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B program_all_FPGA_AL9.sh
.RB [ NORESET ]
.RB [ NOKILL ]
.I bitfile
.RI [ dryrun ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B program_all_FPGA_AL9.sh
is the step that the shifter firmware procedure
.RI ( mu2edaq-documentation/shifter/docs/experts/daq/flash-firmware.md )
runs on every CFO, RTF and DTC node.
It loads a Vivado
.I .bit
file into the FPGA configuration memory over JTAG.
This configuration is volatile: it is lost at the next power cycle, when the
FPGA boots from its flash again.
To change the image the FPGA boots from, use
.BR program_flash_all_FPGA_AL9.sh (1).
.PP
There is no confirmation prompt.
The script works in three steps.
.IP 1. 4
It scans
.I /sys/bus/usb/devices
for USB JTAG adapters whose
.IR idVendor : idProduct
is
.B 0403:6014
(FTDI, reported as "Future Tech" by
.IR find_JTAG_usb_AL9.sh ),
.B 03fd:0013
or
.B 03fd:0008
(Xilinx).
.IP 2. 4
For the
.IR N th
match, counting from 0 in sysfs order, it runs
.EX
/home/mu2ehwdev/program_one_FPGA_AL9.sh NORESET N bitfile
.EE
.IP "" 4
That script takes
.IR /tmp/mu2e.lock ,
changes directory to
.IR /home/mu2ehwdev ,
deletes the Vivado logs there, appends one line to
.IR /home/mu2ehwdev/.Xil/vivado_user_log.txt ,
and runs
.B vivado_lab \-mode batch
with
.IR program_one_FPGA.tcl .
The Tcl script opens hardware target
.I N
(an index into Vivado's
.BR get_hw_targets )
and programs device 0 on it.
The
.I program_one_FPGA_AL9.sh
path is hard-coded.
The copy in
.I /home/mu2ehwdev
runs, not the copy next to this script.
.IP 3. 4
Unless
.B NORESET
is given, it runs
.B sudo
.IB dir /reset_PCIe_AL9.sh
(with
.B NOKILL
if given).
.I dir
is this script's directory, after resolving one level of symbolic link.
By default, therefore, the script kills every
.B xdaq.exe
and
.B boardreader
process on the node, and every process holding
.IR /dev/mu2e* ,
and reloads the
.B mu2e
driver.
See
.BR reset_PCIe_AL9.sh (1).
.PP
Steps 1 and 2 match USB adapters to Vivado hardware targets by position only.
Every target receives the same bitstream, so the order does not matter, but
the count does.
If the sysfs scan finds a different number of adapters than Vivado lists
targets, a target is left unprogrammed or Vivado reports an error for a
missing index.
.SH OPTIONS
The keywords are positional and case-sensitive.
They are matched only in the order shown.
.TP
.B NORESET
Must be the first argument.
Skips step 3.
The FPGAs are reprogrammed, but the
.B mu2e
driver and the PCIe link are not reset, so nothing running on the node is
killed.
The new firmware is not usable through
.I /dev/mu2e*
until a later PCIe reset.
.TP
.B NOKILL
Must be the first argument, or the second after
.BR NORESET .
It is passed through to
.BR reset_PCIe_AL9.sh (1),
which then skips its process kills.
It has no effect together with
.BR NORESET .
.B NOKILL NORESET
in that order is rejected, with "No bit file NORESET found".
.TP
.I bitfile
The bitstream.
The existence check runs relative to the caller's working directory, but
.I program_one_FPGA_AL9.sh
changes to
.I /home/mu2ehwdev
before Vivado opens the file.
A relative path therefore works only when the caller is in
.IR /home/mu2ehwdev .
Use an absolute path.
.TP
.I dryrun
Any non-empty second positional argument (the source comment suggests
.BR d ).
The USB scan still runs, but the program and reset commands are only
printed.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /home/mu2ehwdev/program_one_FPGA_AL9.sh
Per-target programmer, called by absolute path.
.TP
.I /home/xilinx/Vivado_Lab/2021.2/settings64.sh
Vivado Lab environment, sourced by
.IR program_one_FPGA_AL9.sh .
.TP
.I /home/mu2ehwdev/.Xil/vivado_user_log.txt
Append-only record of each FPGA load (date, script, user, host, JTAG index,
bitfile).
It is the only record the scripts keep.
.TP
.I /tmp/mu2e.lock
Symbolic link to the holder's PID.
It is shared with
.BR reset_PCIe_AL9.sh (1)
and with
.I read_dtc_temps.sh
in mu2edaq-operations.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.B HOSTNAME
and
.B USER
appear only in messages.
No variable changes the script's behaviour.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 1
.I bitfile
is non-empty and is not an existing file.
.TP
.B 0
Every other case.
This includes no JTAG adapter found, Vivado failing to program, and the
.B sudo
reset failing.
The closing line "Found N JTAGs, programmed bitfile and PCIe reset for each!"
is printed unconditionally.
.PP
To check the result, read the Vivado output: a line containing
.B HIGH
is flagged "Look here!", and an
.B ERROR
line is flagged "ERROR!".
Also check the version that
.BR reset_PCIe_AL9.sh (1)
reads from register 0x9004, or run
.BR check_firmware_version.sh (1).
.SH EXAMPLES
CFO, from the shifter procedure (as
.BR mu2eshift ).
This command already resets PCIe and kills processes:
.PP
.EX
/home/mu2ehwdev/program_all_FPGA_AL9.sh \e
    /home/mu2ehwdev/CFO_Firmware/CFO2026Apr15_07_22.1/CFO.bit
.EE
.PP
Reprogram without disturbing running processes, and reset later:
.PP
.EX
/home/mu2ehwdev/program_all_FPGA_AL9.sh NORESET \e
    /home/mu2ehwdev/DTC_Firmware/DTC2026Jun08_08_22.1_CRV_6ROC/DTC.bit
.EE
.PP
Show what would be done:
.PP
.EX
/home/mu2ehwdev/program_all_FPGA_AL9.sh /abs/path/DTC.bit d
.EE
.PP
RTF, from the procedure.
The relative path works because of the
.BR cd :
.PP
.EX
cd /home/mu2ehwdev
\&./program_all_FPGA_AL9.sh NORESET RTF_Firmware/RTF2026Jun10_13_23.1a/RTF.bit
.EE
.SH CAVEATS
Running the script with no arguments does not print usage.
The unquoted test
.B [ ! \-f $BITFILE ]
passes when
.I bitfile
is empty, and
.I program_one_FPGA_AL9.sh
rejects its argument list, so no FPGA is touched.
Step 3 still runs and kills every
.B xdaq.exe
and
.B boardreader
on the node.
.B program_all_FPGA_AL9.sh NOKILL
with no bitfile likewise resets PCIe.
.PP
In the shifter procedure, the CFO, Tracker, CRV and STM sections run this
script without
.BR NORESET ,
and then run
.B sudo reset_PCIe_AL9.sh NOKILL
"when others may be using the system".
By then, the first command has already killed their processes.
.PP
When this script runs over
.B ssh
without a terminal, as in the procedure's calorimeter loop, the
.B sudo
in step 3 needs a passwordless sudoers entry.
Otherwise it fails, and the script still exits 0.
